{
  "version": 1,
  "id": "rom-bipedalis",
  "title": "Rom, um 110 n. Chr. - opus mixtum mit Ziegelbaendern und Ziegelstempel (A3-S01)",
  "_note": [
    "Upward is NEGATIVE yPlates. primitives.rs::course_bottom_plates is -(course+1)*3,",
    "so a step stacked on a 21-plate wall sits at yPlates -21, and a Mosaic of 3005.dat",
    "(a 1x1 BRICK, 3 plates tall - the footprint table has no 1x1 plate) sits at -3 to",
    "rest on the ground rather than hang under it.",
    "The wall is opus mixtum: grey stone facing courses with brick bonding courses",
    "running through them. The bands are flush rather than proud, because a course",
    "offset half a stud in z could not clutch to the course below and validate would",
    "be right to say so."
  ],
  "scale": {
    "studsPerMetre": 20,
    "note": "A bipedalis is two Roman feet square, about 59 cm. One stud is 8 mm, so at 20 studs per metre the wall reads at roughly the height a person could stand beside. The stamp is deliberately enlarged: a real brick stamp is a few centimetres across and would be one stud here, which is a stamp nobody can read."
  },
